+---
+name: meta-harness-handbook
+description: Run one iteration of agent harness evolution for HANDBOOK.md.
+---
+
+# Meta-Harness (HANDBOOK.md)
+
+Run ONE iteration of harness evolution.
+
+**You do NOT run benchmarks.** You analyze results + transcripts, propose one harness variant, and implement it. The outer loop (`meta_harness.py`) handles validation, smoke tests and benchmarking.
+
+## CRITICAL CONSTRAINTS
+
+- You MUST produce 1 new harness variant every iteration
+- Do NOT write "the frontier is optimal" or "stop iterating", or abort early.
+
+### The benchmark's rules (enforced by audits; a violation rejects the candidate or voids its benchmark)
+
+HANDBOOK.md drops an office-assistant agent into a company environment with a long
+rule handbook (PDF/Word/HTML) in `/workdir`, a cluttered inbox, Slack, Jira, files.
+Every task's handbook is a distinct modification of one of ten base documents, so
+**the rules cannot be memorized: the worker must find, read and apply them
+in-episode, with its tools.** The environment and its tools are the benchmark's;
+the agent harness around the model is yours.
+
+- **Everything the model gets from the environment comes through a tool call the
+  model issued.** The harness never opens `/workdir`, `/data`, `/initial_data`,
+  `/tests` or `/logs` itself; it never pre-loads the handbook into the context or
+  hands the model a summary, index or section selection it computed on its own.
+  In-episode, model-initiated document tooling IS allowed: a tool the model calls
+  (`search_document`, `outline`, PDF/Word-to-text conversion of a file the model
+  opened) that runs on the model's request, on the model's chosen file, and returns
+  what the model asked for. The model still decides what to read and applies the
+  rules itself.
+- **No handbook, instruction or verifier text in the harness.** Not a rule, a
+  threshold, a deadline, a template, a company/person/product name — in prompts,
+  code strings, comments or docstrings. Validation audits every literal and
+  comment of your module against every task's documents (8-word overlap, single-
+  family names) and rejects on a hit.
+- **Never point the worker outside its workspace and services.** No paths other
+  than `/workdir` in prompts; no `/tests`, `/initial_data`, `/logs`, `/data`,
+  verifier or rubric files anywhere in code. A benchmark whose trials touch any of
+  these is voided (score 0).
+- **The tool-call budget (200) may be lowered, never raised.** The environment,
+  the MCP tool set and the worker model are fixed.
+- **You must not open the task directories** (handbooks, environments, verifiers,
+  rubrics) of the benchmark checkout, or the leak-audit corpus. Your evidence is the
+  run's own outputs under `jobs/` and `logs/`: scores, transcripts, and the
+  measurement reports when the task prompt provides them.
+- **No task-specific hints.** Never mention task names, industries as identifiers,
+  or file names you saw in one transcript. The test: would this harness help an
+  office assistant at any company, with any handbook?
+
+## CONTEXT
+
+You are evolving the **agent harness** around a fixed worker (named in the task
+prompt) on HANDBOOK.md. The baseline is the benchmark's own OpenHands loop with its
+office-assistant prompt: `agents/baseline_openhands.py`, a subclass of
+`HandbookRunner` with no overrides beyond the prompt text.
+
+**The search space is arbitrary Python code.** `HandbookRunner` (`handbook_runner.py`
+in the working directory; read it) runs inside the task container on the OpenHands
+SDK and exposes one method per decision:
+
+- `system_prompt()` — the system message (keep the task's date sentence: `self.date_sentence()`).
+- `instruction()` — the user message the episode starts with (the task instruction; you may wrap it).
+- `llm_kwargs()` / `build_llm(usage_id)` — sampling parameters, timeouts, retries; the LLM object.
+- `build_condenser()` — context policy (the SDK's summarizing condenser, your own, or None).
+- `extra_tools()` — additional `openhands.sdk` Tool objects the model may call in-episode
+  (notes/scratchpad, a document search over a file the model opened, a checklist tool).
+- `on_event(event)` — every conversation event (tool calls, observations, messages, errors).
+- `build_conversation(agent)` / `run_loop(conversation)` — the episode itself: send
+  follow-up messages between runs, add verification passes, re-read steps, a final
+  self-check; `conversation.send_message()` + `conversation.run()` may be called repeatedly.
+- `max_iterations()` — the tool-call budget (<= 200).
+
+Import `openhands.sdk` **only inside methods** (as the base does): your module is also
+imported on the host, where the SDK is absent, to validate the class and declarations.
+The SDK's own extension points (custom `Tool`/`ToolDefinition` + executor, `Agent`,
+`Conversation`, condensers, `LLM`) are available in the benchmark image's runner venv.
+
+Episodes are long: the worker lists `/workdir`, reads the handbook in through its tools
+(PDF/Word/file readers or Python), then acts through mail, Slack, Jira, calendar and
+file tools while the context grows.
+
+**Scoring.** Each task has a rubric of expected-output and prohibited-behaviour checks
+with partial credit; the objective is the mean rubric score (0..1) over all trials; the
+strict pass rate (every check) is reported beside it. Single-run scores are noisy (same
+harness, same task), which is why each candidate gets several trials per task and why
+one task's swing is not evidence.
+
+**Key files to read:**
+
+- `agents/baseline_openhands.py` — the baseline and the `prompt_sections` / `feedback_sections` exemplars.
+- `handbook_runner.py` — every overridable method, with the baseline implementation.
+
+## CANDIDATE DESIGN
+
+Each candidate is a **single Python file** at `agents/<name>.py` containing
+`class AgentHarness(HandbookRunner)` (import it as `from handbook_runner import
+HandbookRunner`) plus the module-level `prompt_sections` / `feedback_sections`
+declarations. No subdirectories. The loop uploads your file and the base next to
+each other into every task container and runs `AgentHarness(config).run()`; the
+import path is always `agents.<name>:AgentHarness`.
+
+### What you can and cannot modify
+
+- **CAN**: edit your new `agents/<name>.py` file freely; override any `HandbookRunner` method.
+- **CANNOT**: modify existing agent files, `handbook_runner.py`, `candidate_agent.py`,
+  `meta_harness.py`, `claude_wrapper.py`, `scripts/`, the benchmark checkout, or anything
+  under `jobs/`.
+
+### Design principles
+
+- One mechanism per candidate. Each candidate tests exactly one hypothesis. If you're tempted to add "and also..." -- that's a second candidate.
+- Mechanism-first. Identify a specific failure mode from transcripts (what the worker read, when it acted, what the rubric penalized), then design the harness change that targets it. Never add changes speculatively.
+- Prefer procedure over content: how the worker finds the rules, when it re-reads them, what it verifies before a write, how the handbook stays usable as the context grows, what the harness injects and when.
+- Every string you add to the prompt or inject at run time is harness-authored text: declare it (below) so the measurement tool can attribute the worker's reading to it.
+
+### REQUIRED in every candidate: declare `prompt_sections`
+
+A downstream white-box tool measures how much the worker READS each sentence of
+its prompt. Every candidate module MUST carry:
+
+```python
+def prompt_sections(system_prompt: str) -> list[tuple[str, str]] | None:
+    """Return (section_name, exact_text) pairs, in order, over the RENDERED
+    prompt (the task's date sentence first, then your text). Each text MUST
+    appear verbatim in system_prompt; together the spans must cover >= 80%;
+    >= 3 sections, one per distinct instruction."""
+```
+
+Copy the implementation from `agents/baseline_openhands.py` (it splits the prompt
+into sentences and names them `date`, `role`, `workspace`, `no-ask`, `begin`) and
+keep it in step with your text: name each span after the instruction it carries.
+The prompt it is checked against is what `AgentHarness(config).system_prompt()`
+returns on a sample task. Rules:
+
+- **Self-contained function.** Constants and imports inside the body, stdlib only.
+- **Enforced at candidate validation.** The validator renders your prompt with a
+  sample date, runs your declaration, and REJECTS the candidate if the function is
+  missing, raises, returns non-verbatim spans, covers < 80%, or returns < 3 sections.
+- **Used per trial at ingest** on the actually-logged prompt; an invalid declaration
+  falls back to a plain sentence split there.
+
+### OPTIONAL companion: declare `feedback_sections`
+
+If your harness injects text at run time — reminders, verification prompts,
+headers on tool results, follow-up messages in `run_loop` — declare it, or it is
+indistinguishable from environment output downstream:
+
+```python
+def feedback_sections(observation_text: str) -> list[tuple[str, str]] | None:
+    """Label the HARNESS-AUTHORED spans inside one tool result or user message
+    as (name, exact_text) pairs, each text verbatim in observation_text. Return
+    None or [] when the message contains no harness text (the common case)."""
+```
+
+Same self-containment rules as `prompt_sections`. Validation probes it (must not
+raise; spans verbatim); ingest applies it per message (`fb:<k>:<name>` rows in
+the digest), so a wrong declaration can only leave your text untagged.
+
+## WORKFLOW
+
+### Step 1: Analyze (1 subagent)
+
+Launch ONE Agent subagent (subagent_type: "general-purpose") **in the foreground** (`run_in_background: false`) and wait for its report. This session is non-interactive: if you end your turn while a subagent is still running in the background, the whole iteration ends with nothing filed. It should:
+
+1. Read state files:
+   - `frontier_val.json` - current best harness per task (path given in task prompt)
+   - `evolution_summary.jsonl` - what's been tried, what worked/didn't, plus `rollout_metrics` (path given in task prompt)
+   - **worker read-maps (only if the task prompt has a "Worker read-maps" section):**
+     `logs/<run>/reports/vitals_iteration_<NN>.md` (consolidated, latest = previous
+     iteration) and `jobs/<run>/<job>/vitals_digest.md` (+ `.parquet`) in each
+     benchmarked agent's job directory, including the frontier's and your own previous
+     candidates'. What they contain: 3b.
+2. **Deep-read weak AND strong trials.** Most important step. Each trial's
+   `agent/completions/*.json` holds, per LLM call, the exact request and the response
+   with the worker's hidden `reasoning_content` and its tool calls; `verifier/reward.txt`
+   holds the score; `agent/run-openhands.log` is the readable transcript. Read the
+   reasoning around the writes (emails, Slack posts, Jira updates, file edits) to see
+   whether the worker looked the rule up in the handbook, recalled it, or guessed. Use the
+   current run outputs under `jobs/` and `logs/` as the only source of truth; never open
+   the benchmark's task directories.
+3. Read agent modules in `agents/*.py`
+3b. **Worker read-maps (only if the task prompt has a "Worker read-maps"
+   section).** After every benchmark a subsample of the harness's trials is
+   replayed through a copy of the same model, recording, turn by turn, how the
+   worker's attention was spent over every section of its context: your harness
+   sentence by sentence (the spans `prompt_sections()` declares), the task
+   instruction, the tool schemas, tool results, the worker's own earlier
+   messages, and — from the turn it read the file in — the handbook's passages.
+   The result is a `vitals_digest.md` (+ `.parquet`) in each agent's job
+   directory and a consolidated report per iteration. Four numbers per
+   section:
+
+   - **`lift` (read lift)** = the attention a section received divided by
+     what it would get if attention were spread evenly over the context.
+     1.0 = read like average text; below 1 = under-read; above 1 = studied.
+   - **`peak` (peak lift)** = the same for the section's 8 hottest content
+     tokens. A passage with a low lift and a high peak is being looked up
+     for one line, not skipped.
+   - **`consult`** (consult ratio) and **`contrast`** = how much MORE the
+     worker looks at a section at the moments it is uncertain (1.0 = no
+     difference; above 1 = read more when unsure; below 1 = read while
+     confident, ignored when stuck).
+   - `share` (total attention), `footprint_tok` (size), `*_z_role` (vs every
+     other design's sections of the same role).
+
+   Each digest opens with headlines and a **per-trial map**: task, score, the
+   parent's score on the same task, turns, prompt size, the pooled lift of the
+   harness / the instruction / the handbook at the turns that act (writes and
+   python/bash), the hottest handbook passage there with its hot line, a
+   `read_once` flag (read the handbook on entry, never went back), and entropy
+   early vs late. Start there. The raw per-token store and its loader are named
+   in the task prompt if you need more than the digest.
+
+   **Read-maps are evidence, never the objective.** Attention is where the
+   model looked, not proof of what it used: a rule read intensely can still be
+   misapplied, and an under-read passage may have been applied from memory.
+   Nothing here is scored; the objective is the rubric score. Rules for using
+   them:
+   - A hypothesis must predict a *score* change through a mechanism. A change
+     whose only predicted effect is a reading statistic (more lift on the
+     handbook, a hotter harness sentence) is not a candidate.
+   - A read-map observation drives a candidate only when a transcript
+     corroborates it: cite the trial and turn where the worker's reasoning or
+     action shows the mechanism. If the transcripts already explain the
+     failures, you do not need the read-maps at all.
+   - Do not tune to the digest: no candidate should exist to move a number in
+     this table. If a read-map shapes a hypothesis, say so in `evidence`.
+
+   Each digest also carries a **`Delta vs parent`** block: the harness diffed
+   token by token against the agent its `base_system` names (default: the
+   frontier agent), on the same tasks, with the read lift of the text the edit
+   added, removed and kept, and the two agents' scores per task. Name the agent
+   you edited in `base_system` (optional field of pending_eval.json) so the
+   delta is computed against it.
+
+4. Return:
+
+```
+STATE: <5-line summary: current scores, what's been tried, avg tokens/tool calls/cost>
+
+HYPOTHESIS: "<falsifiable claim about what will improve scores>"
+CANDIDATE: name=<snake_case>, changes="<specific changes>", prediction="<expected score change AND expected token/tool-call impact>"
+```
+
+### Step 2: Implement (1 subagent)
+
+Launch **1 Agent subagent** (subagent_type: "general-purpose"). The prompt must include the candidate name, the exact changes from Step 1, the working directory, and the validation command from the task prompt.
+
+The subagent should:
+
+1. Copy `agents/baseline_openhands.py` (or another agent in `agents/`) to `agents/<snake_case_name>.py`. Never import from other candidate agents; copy what you reuse.
+2. Implement the mechanism by overriding `HandbookRunner` methods; keep `prompt_sections` (and `feedback_sections`, if you inject text) in step with every string the worker will see.
+3. **Validate** with the command given in the task prompt (`... meta_harness.py --validate-agent agents.<name>:AgentHarness`, from the working directory). It imports the class on the host, renders the prompt on a sample task, checks the declarations and the budget, and runs the code leak audit (strings, comments, file access). Fix and re-run until it prints `OK`. The loop then smoke-tests the candidate on one task before benchmarking.
+4. Return: file path, validation output
+
+### Step 3: Write pending_eval.json
+
+Write `pending_eval.json` to the path specified in the task prompt:
+
+```json
+{
+  "iteration": <N>,
+  "candidates": [
+    {
+      "name": "<name>",
+      "import_path": "agents.<name>:AgentHarness",
+      "hypothesis": "<falsifiable claim>",
+      "changes": "<what was changed>",
+      "base_system": "<the agent this edits (its name, optional); the delta is measured against it>",
+      "evidence": "<which sources drove the hypothesis — scores, transcripts (trial + turn), or the measurement reports if provided — one line each>",
+      "expected_efficiency": "<expected token/tool-call impact>"
+    }
+  ]
+}
+```
+
+Output: `CANDIDATES: <name1>`
+
+## IMPORTANT NOTES
+
+- **Always name the class `AgentHarness`**; the import path is always `agents.<name>:AgentHarness`.
+- The harness runs in the container with the OpenHands runner venv; anything it needs
+  must be in that venv (openhands-sdk, litellm, pydantic, stdlib) or in your file.
+- `self.config` holds the task's `systemPrompt` (date sentence first), `instruction`,
+  `model`, `mcpUrl`, `maxToolCalls`, `llmKwargs`, `logDir`. Keep `log_completions` on.
+- Transcripts contain the handbook text the worker read. You may read it to
+  understand a failure; you may never copy any of it, or anything derived from a
+  specific handbook, into a candidate.
