+## WORKER READ-MAPS (only when the task prompt has a "Worker read-maps" section)
+
+These runs replay a sample of every benchmarked agent's trials through a reference model and record, turn by turn, how attention was spread over the sections of the worker's context. The replay teacher-forces the logged turns through an open-weight reference model, which may differ from the worker model. Two declarations in the agent file tell the replay which text is which.
+
+### Required: `prompt_sections`
+
+Every candidate agent file must define a module-level function:
+
+```python
+def prompt_sections(system_prompt: str) -> list[tuple[str, str]] | None:
+    """Return (section_name, exact_text) pairs, in order, labeling the
+    authored scaffold (everything before 'Current terminal state:'). Each
+    text must appear verbatim in system_prompt; together the spans must
+    cover >= 80% of the authored region; >= 4 sections, one per distinct
+    functional block."""
+```
+
+Copy the implementation from `agents/baseline_kira.py` (sections `task_framing`, `autonomy_policy`, `completion_check`, `task_description`) and update it whenever the candidate adds, removes, or rewords scaffold text: a new template block, an injected environment bootstrap, a skills catalogue, memory or context injection, rewritten instructions. Name sections by function (`env_bootstrap`, `skills_catalogue`, `memory_context`, `tool_spec:<tool>`, `directive:<n>`).
+
+- **Self-contained.** Constants and imports (stdlib or `prompt_provenance` only) go inside the function body. The function is extracted from the file by AST and executed where harbor is not importable, so module-level globals are not available.
+- **Enforced at validation.** The validator renders the prompt template, runs the declaration on it, and rejects the candidate if the function is missing, raises, returns spans that are not verbatim, covers < 80% of the authored region, or returns fewer than 4 sections.
+- **Checked again per trial** against the logged prompt. A declaration that no longer matches falls back to coarse structural sections there, which costs measurement resolution.
+
+### Optional: `feedback_sections`
+
+If the candidate injects text into observations at run time (retry nudges, completion checklists, truncation warnings, tool-result headers), declare it; otherwise that text is counted as terminal output:
+
+```python
+def feedback_sections(observation_text: str) -> list[tuple[str, str]] | None:
+    """Label the harness-authored spans inside one observation as
+    (name, exact_text) pairs, each verbatim in observation_text. The
+    untagged remainder is environment output. Return None or [] when the
+    observation contains no harness text."""
+```
+
+Copy the implementation from `agents/baseline_kira.py` and extend it with the templates your mechanism injects. The same self-containment rules apply. Validation probes it on synthetic observations, and ingestion validates it per observation, so a wrong declaration can only leave text untagged.
+
+Tell the implementation subagent (Step 2) to keep both declarations current.
+
+### Reading the digests
+
+Paths are in the task prompt. Each row of a digest is a section of the worker's context: the scaffold sections from `prompt_sections()`, injected feedback from `feedback_sections()` pooled by template (`fb:<name>`), and environment observations pooled by age (`obs[fresh]`, `obs[1-3]`, `obs[4+]`). Values pool over the replayed turns.
+
+- `footprint_tok`: tokens the section costs. `mass_share`: total attention it drew.
+- `lift` (read lift): attention per token relative to an average context token. 1.0 = average, 0.3 = mostly skipped, 4.0 = studied.
+- `peak` (peak lift): the same for the section's 8 most-read content tokens (sections of 32+ tokens). Low lift with a high peak means one line is being looked up; low lift with a low peak means the section is unread.
+- `consult_ratio`: the section's attention share weighted by the model's output entropy, over its plain share. 1.0 = no difference, above 1 = read more when unsure, below 1 = read while confident.
+- `contrast` (consult contrast): the section's share at the 10% most uncertain steps over its share at the 10% most confident.
+- `persists` (feedback rows): in how many trials the harness re-injected the same span on 3+ consecutive turns. Feedback that keeps being re-injected was read and not acted on, which lift cannot show.
+- `lift_z_role` / `consult_z_role`: each against the norm for that role across all agents so far.
+- A second table follows the scaffold sections by turn bucket: is a directive absorbed early, re-read, or fixated on late?
+
+Each digest opens with section headlines for both measures, then a per-trial map: task, outcome, turns, seconds, timeout, the parent agent's numbers on the same task, the pooled lift of the scaffold, the freshest observation and the injected feedback, the scaffold's consult ratio and the section consulted most when unsure, and the entropy early versus late in the trial. Start there.
+
+The `Delta vs parent` block diffs the agent's first-turn prompt token by token against the agent its `base_system` names (default: the frontier agent) on the same tasks. It gives the read lift of the text the edit added, removed, and kept (added and removed text is read against the retained text in the same prompt, so an edit is judged on whether its own text got read), plus both agents' pass rate, turns and seconds per task.
+
+The raw per-token stores behind the digests, their schema and a standalone loader are listed in the task prompt, for questions the digests do not answer.
+
+### Rules
+
+Read-maps are evidence, never the objective. Attention shows where the model looked, not what it used, and nothing in the digests is scored. Pass rate remains the only objective.
+
+- A hypothesis must predict a pass-rate change through a mechanism. A change whose only predicted effect is a reading statistic is not a candidate.
+- A read-map observation drives a candidate only when a trajectory corroborates it: cite the trial and turn where the worker's reasoning or action shows the mechanism. If the trajectories already explain the failures, you do not need the read-maps.
+- Do not tune to the digest: no candidate should exist to move a number in these tables.
+
+Add two fields to each candidate in `pending_eval.json`:
+
+```json
+"base_system": "<the agent this candidate edits; its Delta vs parent is computed against it>",
+"evidence": "<what drove the hypothesis: scores, trajectories (trial + turn), read-maps; one line each>"
+```
+
 ## IMPORTANT NOTES
 
 - **Always name the class `AgentHarness`** in candidate files. The import path is always `agents.<name>:AgentHarness`.
diff --git a/reference_examples/terminal_bench_2/agents/baseline_kira.py b/reference_examples/terminal_bench_2/agents/baseline_kira.py
index 732c4f0..cb4aef7 100644
--- a/reference_examples/terminal_bench_2/agents/baseline_kira.py
+++ b/reference_examples/terminal_bench_2/agents/baseline_kira.py
@@ -58,6 +58,87 @@ BLOCK_TIMEOUT_SEC = 600  # 10 minutes
 _MARKER_PREFIX = "__CMDEND__"  # Marker prefix for command completion detection
 
 
+def prompt_sections(system_prompt: str) -> list[tuple[str, str]] | None:
+    """Label the authored scaffold blocks of the rendered first prompt.
+
+    Return (section_name, exact_text) pairs in order of appearance. Every
+    text must occur verbatim in ``system_prompt``, and together the spans
+    must cover >= 80% of the authored region (everything before "Current
+    terminal state:"), one span per functional block, >= 4 spans.
+
+    Keep this function self-contained (constants and any stdlib imports in
+    the body): it is extracted from this file by AST and executed where
+    harbor is not importable. Update it whenever scaffold text is added,
+    removed, or reworded.
+    """
+    markers = [
+        ("task_framing",
+         "You are an AI assistant tasked with solving command-line tasks"),
+        ("autonomy_policy", "Your plan MUST account"),
+        ("completion_check",
+         "Before calling task_complete, verify minimal state changes:"),
+        ("task_description", "Task Description:"),
+    ]
+    end = system_prompt.find("Current terminal state:")
+    authored = system_prompt[:end] if end >= 0 else system_prompt
+    marks = []
+    for name, marker in markers:
+        i = authored.find(marker)
+        if i >= 0:
+            marks.append((i, name))
+    if len(marks) < len(markers):
+        return None
+    marks.sort()
+    out = []
+    for k, (pos, name) in enumerate(marks):
+        stop = marks[k + 1][0] if k + 1 < len(marks) else len(authored)
+        text = authored[pos:stop].strip()
+        if text:
+            out.append((name, text))
+    return out
+
+
+def feedback_sections(observation_text: str) -> list[tuple[str, str]] | None:
+    """Label the harness-authored spans inside one observation.
+
+    Covers the text this harness injects at run time (completion
+    confirmation, truncation warning, file/image read framing). Return
+    (name, exact_text) pairs, each verbatim in ``observation_text``; the
+    untagged remainder is environment output. Return None when the
+    observation contains no harness text. Same rules as prompt_sections.
+    """
+    out: list[tuple[str, str]] = []
+    obs = observation_text
+
+    # _get_completion_confirmation_message: task echo + fixed checklist
+    i = obs.find("Original task:\n")
+    j = obs.find("\n\nCurrent terminal state:\n", i) if i >= 0 else -1
+    if i >= 0 and j > i:
+        out.append(("confirm_task_echo", obs[i:j]))
+    head = "Are you sure you want to mark the task as complete?"
+    tail = "call task_complete tool again."
+    i = obs.find(head)
+    j = obs.find(tail, i) if i >= 0 else -1
+    if i >= 0 and j > i:
+        out.append(("confirm_checklist", obs[i:j + len(tail)]))
+
+    trunc = ("ERROR!! Your response was truncated. "
+             "Please provide a shorter response with fewer commands.")
+    if trunc in obs:
+        out.append(("truncation_warning", trunc))
+
+    # _execute_image_read result framing
+    for name, prefix in [("file_read_header", "File Read Result for '"),
+                         ("image_read_error", "ERROR: Unsupported image format '"),
+                         ("file_read_error", "ERROR: Failed to read file '")]:
+        i = obs.find(prefix)
+        if i >= 0:
+            j = obs.find("\n", i)
+            out.append((name, obs[i:j if j > i else len(obs)]))
+
+    return out or None
+
